+    cablespeakerwavtable.h
+
+Abstract:
+
+    LoopIt7 Cable, not part of Microsoft's sample. The wave filter behind "Cable In", the
+    playback end of the cable.
+
+    Deliberately smaller than the sample's speaker filter, which it replaces:
+
+    - One system pin instance and no offload pin, so exactly one render stream can exist.
+      The sample's speaker is offload-capable, which lets Windows open several render
+      streams at once, and every one of them would write into the cable.
+
+    - No hardware loopback pin. With no offload, the audio engine serves WASAPI loopback
+      itself, so a loopback tap on the cable cannot compete with "Cable Out" for audio.
+
+    - RAW is the only processing mode. Windows opens a pin per mode a driver declares; with
+      one mode it mixes everything into one stream, and a cable should apply no processing.
+
+    - Exactly one format, 48 kHz / 16-bit / stereo PCM, matching "Cable Out" (see
+      micinwavtable.h). The ring copies bytes; it cannot resample, so both ends must agree.
+
+    Same pin layout as the sample's Bluetooth HFP speaker (KSPIN_WAVE_RENDER3_*), which is
+    the layout CMiniportWaveRT expects from a render filter that is not offload-capable.
+
+--*/
+
